    {
      "id": "sol.ahsme-1964-p11.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Writing both equations with common bases gives \\[ \\begin{aligned} x&=3y+3,\\\\ 2y&=x-9. \\end{aligned} \\] Substitution yields \\(y=6\\) and \\(x=21,\\) so \\(x+y=27.\\)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is D.",
      "hints": [
        "Rewrite \\(8\\) as \\(2^3\\) and \\(9\\) as \\(3^2\\)",
        "Equate exponents to obtain two linear equations in \\(x\\) and \\(y\\)"
